    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caral">Caral</a>–Supe was a cluster of as many as thirty population centres in three small valleys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>’s north-central coast, flourishing between about 3000 and 1800 BCE.</li><li>Its people raised great stepped platform mounds and sunken circular plazas, grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and food crops by irrigation and ate fish from the coast, yet made no pottery and almost no art.</li><li>There is no evidence of warfare, and archaeologists still debate how it was governed and whether the sea or farming fed its rise.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caral">Caral</a>–Supe builders hauled stones to their pyramids in loosely meshed bags of a highland grass, shicra, packing the filled bags behind each retaining wall; radiocarbon dates of these bags date Caral to about 2627 BCE.</p></aside>
    </section>

